WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.302 --> 00:00:07.555
In the library that morning, Mr. Rochester informed me that we were to be married in four weeks.

2
00:00:07.556 --> 00:00:13.781
"Why did you take such pains to make me believe that you wished to marry Miss Ingram?" I asked.

3
00:00:13.782 --> 00:00:22.281
"I wanted to make you fall as madly in love with me as I was with you. I knew jealousy was my best ally in that cause."

4
00:00:22.282 --> 00:00:29.949
I didn't think this was fair to Miss Ingram, but he explained again that she had been more in love with his money than with him.

5
00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:35.957
I asked him to tell Mrs. Fairfax that we were engaged when she saw us kissing last night.

6
00:00:35.958 --> 00:00:40.858
He did so immediately, but I still felt that she didn't approve.

7
00:00:40.859 --> 00:00:47.016
"Gentlemen don't usually marry their governesses," she sniffed.

8
00:00:47.017 --> 00:00:53.759
That morning Mr. Rochester took me to Millcote to purchase some new clothes and jewelry.

9
00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:58.847
The more he bought me, the more my cheeks burned with annoyance and shame.

10
00:00:58.848 --> 00:01:09.165
Suddenly I remembered my uncle, John Eyre, and his intention to adopt me and make me his heir. I decided to write to him about my marriage.

11
00:01:09.166 --> 00:01:16.983
Perhaps one day he would leave me an inheritance, and then I would be able to approach Mr. Rochester as an equal.

12
00:01:16.984 --> 00:01:22.807
Four weeks passed very quickly, and it was now the night before my wedding.

13
00:01:22.808 --> 00:01:30.576
Mr. Rochester had been away from home on business since the previous day, and I was still waiting for him to return.

14
00:01:30.577 --> 00:01:35.560
I desperately needed to speak to him about something that had occurred the previous night.

15
00:01:35.561 --> 00:01:43.735
A strong wind had been blowing all day. I went outside and ran through the orchard with the wind at my back.

16
00:01:43.736 --> 00:01:49.566
When I came to the wreck of the chestnut tree, the moon appeared momentarily.

17
00:01:49.567 --> 00:01:54.421
Half-covered by clouds, its disk was the color of blood.

18
00:01:54.422 --> 00:02:02.114
I went back to the house to make sure that a fire had been lit in the library in anticipation of Mr. Rochester's arrival.

19
00:02:02.114 --> 00:02:10.607
Feeling more restless than ever, I ran down to the gates. Tears filled my eyes when it began to rain.

20
00:02:10.608 --> 00:02:15.786
"I wish he would come!" I exclaimed, seized with foreboding.

21
00:02:15.787 --> 00:02:21.540
I opened the gates, determined to walk until I met him, and soon enough I did.

22
00:02:21.541 --> 00:02:26.943
He pulled me up onto his horse, and together we rode back to Thornfield.

23
00:02:26.944 --> 00:02:32.682
After we had changed into dry clothes, I met Mr. Rochester in the library.

24
00:02:32.683 --> 00:02:41.418
"What a bright spot of color you have on each cheek," he said, "and how strangely your eyes glitter! Are you well?"

25
00:02:41.419 --> 00:02:48.911
"I believe I am, but I must tell you about yesterday. All day I was very busy and very happy.

26
00:02:48.912 --> 00:02:53.506
It's a glorious thing to think about living with you because I love you."

27
00:02:53.507 --> 00:02:58.480
Here he wanted to kiss me, but I needed to press on with my story.

28
00:02:58.481 --> 00:03:05.829
"At sunset Adele and her nurse, Sophie, called me upstairs to look at my wedding dress, which had just arrived

29
00:03:05.830 --> 00:03:13.326
Underneath the dress I also found your gift, the extravagantly embroidered veil, which you had sent for from London."

30
00:03:13.327 --> 00:03:23.220
"You look so mournful," said Mr. Rochester. "Did you find something in the veil besides its embroidery—poison or a dagger perhaps?"

31
00:03:23.221 --> 00:03:33.876
"No, sir. Beyond the delicacy and richness of the fabric, I found nothing except your pride, and that did not scare me because I'm used to that."

32
00:03:33.877 --> 00:03:43.961
I continued with my story. For some time after I'd gone to bed, I could not sleep, and when I did sleep, I had a series of bad dreams.

33
00:03:43.962 --> 00:03:50.871
One was so upsetting that I woke up. I saw a candle burning on my dressing table.

34
00:03:50.872 --> 00:03:55.436
The door to my closet was open, and I heard a rustling within.

35
00:03:55.437 --> 00:04:03.354
Before going to bed, I had hung my wedding dress and veil there, so I thought it was morning and Sophie had come to help me dress.

36
00:04:03.355 --> 00:04:11.774
I called Sophie's name. No one answered, but a figure emerged from the closet and held the light aloft.

37
00:04:11.775 --> 00:04:21.332
"My blood ran cold, Mr. Rochester, because this was not Sophie or Mrs. Fairfax or Leah or even that strange woman, Grace Poole."

38
00:04:21.333 --> 00:04:25.062
"It must have been one of them," he insisted.

39
00:04:25.063 --> 00:04:31.744
"I solemnly assure you that I have never seen this creature before at Thornfield."

40
00:04:31.745 --> 00:04:33.920
"Describe her, Jane."

41
00:04:33.921 --> 00:04:41.083
"She was a tall, large woman with long, dark hair and dressed all in white."

42
00:04:41.084 --> 00:04:43.153
"Did you see her face?"

43
00:04:43.153 --> 00:04:58.223
"Not at first, but then she took my veil from the closet. She held it up, gazed at it for a long time, threw it over her head, and turned to the mirror. At that moment I saw her reflection quite clearly."

44
00:04:58.224 --> 00:05:00.779
"And what did she look like?"

45
00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:11.143
"I never saw such a ghastly face! It was discolored—purple with dark and swollen lips, a furrowed brow, and bloodshot eyes."

46
00:05:11.144 --> 00:05:16.121
"Ghosts are usually pale, Jane. What did this creature do?"

47
00:05:16.122 --> 00:05:24.270
"Sir, she removed my veil from her gaunt head, ripped the veil in two, and then trampled the pieces on the floor."

48
00:05:24.271 --> 00:05:26.548
"And then?"

49
00:05:26.549 --> 00:05:35.294
"She picked up the candle and retreated to the door. Just at my bedside, she thrust the candle up close to my face and blew it out.

50
00:05:35.295 --> 00:05:43.868
And for only the second time in my life, I fainted from terror. Now sir, tell me: who was that woman?"

51
00:05:43.868 --> 00:05:48.253
"I don't know, Jane. It must have been a dream."

52
00:05:48.254 --> 00:05:55.014
"That's what I thought, but when I rose this morning, I found my veil on the floor, torn in two from top to bottom."

53
00:05:55.015 --> 00:05:59.750
Mr. Rochester shuddered and hastily threw his arms around me.

54
00:05:59.751 --> 00:06:08.011
"Thank goodness that if anything harmful did come near you last night, it only damaged the veil. Oh, to think what might have happened!"

55
00:06:08.012 --> 00:06:16.671
He held me so tightly that I could hardly breathe. "Now I will explain everything to you," he said.

56
00:06:16.672 --> 00:06:26.252
"A woman did enter your room last night, and it was Grace Poole, but waking from your nightmare, you ascribed to her a goblin appearance.

57
00:06:26.253 --> 00:06:35.055
I know you wonder why I keep her in my house. When we have been married a year and a day, I will tell you—but not now."

58
00:06:35.056 --> 00:06:40.379
His answer seemed to be the only possible one, and it satisfied me.

59
00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:48.546
He didn't want me to sleep alone that night, so he made me promise to sleep with Adele in the nursery and to lock the door securely.

60
00:06:48.547 --> 00:06:55.807
That night I didn't sleep at all. With little Adele in my arms, I waited for the coming day.

61
00:06:55.808 --> 00:07:07.043
She seemed the emblem of my past life while Mr. Rochester was the dreaded but adored symbol of my unknown future.

